 @0xsergy  For those of you who live in the US or EU, even Asia, it may be the best option, but for those of us who live in the third world (LATAM) it is not, the prices are prohibitive, partly because of the devaluation of our currency, and on the other hand because we do not have official distributors but intermediaries, so the most sensible thing in these places is to opt for the 60 or 70 series, since up to that point the prices remain contained, then they shoot up very disproportionately.

But even looking at the US prices, the 70 series looks like the max sensible choice, an 80 or higher is not always the best idea, it often costs twice as much as a 60 and even a 70, and if you play at 1080 or max at 1440, even if you change it in 5 years, still it is more sensible to have a 60 or 70 and change it every 3 years and get the new features.

Anyway, that was until the RTX4000 series came out, the jump between generations has been so mediocre that change a 60 series no more than 2 gens older is not justified right now, and for me who plays at 1080p the 3060Ti is still very good, the truth is that it is the first time that despite having had this card for 4 years, I still do not see the need or justification to change it, and if I did, I would return to AMD again since I am not tied to brands and is more razonable an RX9060 from what I know about this one, although I have to say that I have not had an AMD for more than 6 years, the last one was an 8GB RX480 (8GB in 2017, it is really shameful that they continue to insist on that in 2025).

